(TEST (if #true 1 0) 1 1 1)
(TEST (if #false 1 2) 2 2 2)
(TEST (if "hi" 1 2) error 1 1)
(TEST (if 0 1 2) error 1 2)
(TEST (or 0 "yay") error 0 #t)

The conditionals were a little hard to grasp, but after testing, I can offer insight that the languages differ in how strictly their rules are enforced.
Language 1 is so strict that only true/false is allowed. Everything else seems to crash the test, resulting in an error.
Language 2 is a little looser, meaning that everything is true except for false. This makes only false as false, so 0, "", "0" all behave as true.
Language 3 is greatly customized, where a handful of values are false, and everything else is true. Some values I saw as false are #false, 0, "", "0".
